---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import MasonXMonogram from '../../components/MasonXMonogram.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../../components/TitleBlock.astro';

const title = "What Goes On a Masonry Shop Drawing (and Who Has to Draw It) | TMM Blog";
const description = "A masonry shop drawing is the coursed submittal you have to produce before laying block — coursing, special shapes, reinforcing, control joints. Here's what it has to show.";
const canonical = "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/whats-on-a-masonry-shop-drawing/";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <MasonXMonogram />
  <TitleBlock sheet="B1.6 — What's On a Shop Drawing" ctaLabel="Join Waitlist →" ctaHref="/#waitlist" />

  <div class="section-divider">
    <span>Field Notes · B1.6</span>
    <span class="scale-marks"></span>
    <span>Shop Drawings</span>
  </div>

  <section class="blog-post-hero">
    <div class="blog-post-hero-inner">
      <div class="post-kicker">Field Notes</div>
      <h1>
        What Goes On a Masonry Shop Drawing — and Who Has to
        <span class="accent">Draw It</span>
      </h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span><strong>Ryan Shanley</strong></span>
        <span>June 2026</span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="blog-post-body">

    <p>
      A masonry shop drawing is the coursed elevation that proves the wall before anybody lays a
      block. And here's the part that catches new contractors off guard: it's not the architect's
      drawing. It's yours. You have to make it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The architect gives you design intent — a wall, a height, some openings. The shop drawing is
      where you turn that into the wall as it actually gets built, course by course, and submit it
      for approval before you're allowed to start. Get it right and the job moves. Get it wrong, or
      leave something out, and you wait.
    </p>

    <h2>Who actually draws it</h2>

    <p>
      Construction drawings come from the architect and the engineer. The shop drawing comes from
      you — the masonry contractor, or the detailer you hire. You take their intent and you detail
      how the wall goes together: the coursing, the units, the reinforcing, the joints. Then it
      goes back to the architect and engineer for review.
    </p>

    <p>
      You don't order block or lay a course until it comes back stamped approved. On most jobs that
      review runs two to three weeks. That clock is the reason a missed detail hurts so much — a
      rejected submittal doesn't just cost a redraw, it costs another trip through the queue.
    </p>

    <h2>What it has to show</h2>

    <p>
      A shop drawing the reviewer will approve has to show the wall the way it's built, not the way
      it's billed. That means all of this, coordinated and drawn before you start:
    </p>

    <ul class="page-list">
      <li><strong>Coursing</strong> — every course, the full elevation. Vertical dimensions land on the 8-inch module, and openings and sills line up to it so you're not cutting block just to finish a wall.</li>
      <li><strong>Special shapes</strong> — corners, bond beam, lintel, and sill units, called out where they fall. Not lumped into a field of stretchers.</li>
      <li><strong>Openings</strong> — every door and window with its size and course location, with the lintel and jamb units resolved around it.</li>
      <li><strong>Reinforcing</strong> — rebar size, spacing, and placement in the cores and <a href="/bond-beam-estimating">bond beams</a>, with the cover and lap-splice lengths the code calls for. The reviewer checks these closely.</li>
      <li><strong>Joint reinforcement</strong> — the ladder or truss mesh, with gauge and spacing.</li>
      <li><strong>Control joints</strong> — located and spaced on the elevation (for CMU, generally no more than 25 feet apart).</li>
      <li><strong>Finishes</strong> — if it's architectural block, the split face, ground face, or scored bands shown in their real coursing positions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why it's the slowest paper in the package</h2>

    <p>
      Every one of those has to be right, coordinated with the others, and drawn before you build.
      Do it by hand, or send it to a detailing service, and it's days of work and a fee per sheet —
      plus a full redraw every time the plans change. Miss a detail and the submittal comes back
      rejected, and you're back in the two-to-three-week line. That's why the shop drawing is the
      slowest, most expensive piece of paper on a lot of jobs.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The shop drawing isn't extra work. It's the wall, drawn before you lay it. The only question
        is whether you draw it by hand or the model hands it to you.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>It doesn't have to be a separate job</h2>

    <p>
      If the wall is built course by course in a model — every unit tagged as you draw it — the shop
      drawing falls out of that automatically. Coursing numbered. Openings labeled. Bond beams and
      control joints in position. Finishes banded. That's the whole idea behind
      <a href="/masonry-shop-drawings">masonry shop drawings</a> in The Masonry Modeler: you build
      the wall once, and the submittal draws itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you're detailing every elevation by hand before you're even allowed to start, that's the
      part I'm trying to take off your plate.
    </p>

    <a href="/#waitlist" class="post-cta-inline">Join the waitlist →</a>

  </article>

  <div class="blog-post-footer">
    <a href="/blog/" class="blog-back-link">← All posts</a>
  </div>

</Layout>
